1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,403
<font color="#000000">This is All in a Row: Compressed Convolution
Networks for Graphs,</font>

2
00:00:05,483 --> 00:00:09,697
<font color="#000000">an ICML 2023 paper from the Chinese Academy of Sciences.</font>

3
00:00:09,777 --> 00:00:15,217
<font color="#000000">Can we run standard, CNN-style Euclidean
convolution directly on graphs?</font>

4
00:00:15,297 --> 00:00:20,277
<font color="#000000">Yes: learn a differentiable permutation that lines
nodes in a row,</font>

5
00:00:20,357 --> 00:00:22,730
<font color="#000000">then slide a kernel across them.</font>

6
00:00:22,810 --> 00:00:25,720
<font color="#000000">The model, CoCN, sets state of the art.</font>

7
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:29,321
<font color="#000000">Convolutional graph networks have two weaknesses.</font>

8
00:00:29,401 --> 00:00:32,689
<font color="#000000">Because their filters come from graph polynomials,</font>

9
00:00:32,769 --> 00:00:36,259
<font color="#000000">they lack expressiveness on a small parameter budget.</font>

10
00:00:36,339 --> 00:00:40,503
<font color="#000000">And they cannot learn hierarchical, multi-scale
features alone,</font>

11
00:00:40,583 --> 00:00:44,275
<font color="#000000">needing bolt-on tricks like clustering or node dropping.</font>

12
00:00:44,355 --> 00:00:51,415
<font color="#000000">Image CNNs have neither problem: local learning and
shared filters naturally capture multi-scale patterns.</font>

13
00:00:51,495 --> 00:00:55,120
<font color="#000000">But their Euclidean convolution assumes a regular grid,</font>

14
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:57,410
<font color="#000000">and does not fit irregular graphs.</font>

15
00:00:57,790 --> 00:01:01,099
<font color="#000000">So why not borrow Euclidean convolution for graphs?</font>

16
00:01:01,179 --> 00:01:03,891
<font color="#000000">Convolution is sensitive to spatial order,</font>

17
00:01:03,971 --> 00:01:07,147
<font color="#000000">but graph networks must be permutation invariant.</font>

18
00:01:07,227 --> 00:01:11,933
<font color="#000000">Earlier methods picked a node ordering
independently of the convolution,</font>

19
00:01:12,013 --> 00:01:16,253
<font color="#000000">so it could not be tuned for the task and often
lost information.</font>

20
00:01:16,333 --> 00:01:21,370
<font color="#000000">What is missing is an ordering that is
differentiable and learned end to end.</font>

21
00:01:21,750 --> 00:01:25,246
<font color="#000000">The paper makes two contributions.</font>

22
00:01:25,326 --> 00:01:28,823
<font color="#000000">First,</font>

23
00:01:28,903 --> 00:01:32,399
<font color="#000000">a differentiable regularization for graphs: a
learnable permutation that lines nodes into</font>

24
00:01:32,479 --> 00:01:35,975
<font color="#000000">a single row while preserving permutation invariance,</font>

25
00:01:36,055 --> 00:01:39,551
<font color="#000000">so Euclidean convolution finally applies.</font>

26
00:01:39,631 --> 00:01:43,128
<font color="#000000">Second, the model built on it, the Compressed
Convolution Network, or CoCN.</font>

27
00:01:43,208 --> 00:01:46,704
<font color="#000000">It learns both node and structure features and
trains end to end,</font>

28
00:01:46,784 --> 00:01:50,280
<font color="#000000">beating competitive GNNs and pooling models.</font>

29
00:01:50,660 --> 00:01:54,042
<font color="#000000">Here is how CoCN works.</font>

30
00:01:54,122 --> 00:01:57,503
<font color="#000000">First,</font>

31
00:01:57,583 --> 00:02:00,965
<font color="#000000">permutation generation regresses an absolute
position for each node with an MLP on Laplacian-smoothed features,</font>

32
00:02:01,045 --> 00:02:04,426
<font color="#000000">so similar nodes land nearby.</font>

33
00:02:04,506 --> 00:02:07,888
<font color="#000000">These form a relaxed permutation matrix,</font>

34
00:02:07,968 --> 00:02:11,349
<font color="#000000">controlled by a factor tau that provably converges
to a true permutation.</font>

35
00:02:11,429 --> 00:02:14,811
<font color="#000000">With nodes in a row,</font>

36
00:02:14,891 --> 00:02:18,272
<font color="#000000">CoCN slides a kernel along the diagonal of the
permuted edge and node matrices,</font>

37
00:02:18,352 --> 00:02:21,734
<font color="#000000">extracting node features and their structure together.</font>

38
00:02:21,814 --> 00:02:25,195
<font color="#000000">Compressed pooling builds a hierarchy,</font>

39
00:02:25,275 --> 00:02:28,657
<font color="#000000">and transposed convolution up-samples for predictions.</font>

40
00:02:28,737 --> 00:02:32,118
<font color="#000000">Crucially, this cuts permutation modeling from n
factorial to n squared,</font>

41
00:02:32,198 --> 00:02:35,580
<font color="#000000">letting it scale to large graphs.</font>

42
00:02:35,960 --> 00:02:37,743
<font color="#000000">The evaluation is broad.</font>

43
00:02:37,823 --> 00:02:44,187
<font color="#000000">For graph classification, six datasets: MUTAG,
PROTEINS, NCI1, COLLAB, IMDB-Binary,</font>

44
00:02:44,267 --> 00:02:47,913
<font color="#000000">and IMDB-Multi, under ten-fold cross-validation.</font>

45
00:02:47,993 --> 00:02:54,356
<font color="#000000">For node classification, six heterophilic
benchmarks: Chameleon, Squirrel, Cornell,</font>

46
00:02:54,436 --> 00:02:56,530
<font color="#000000">Texas, Wisconsin, and Actor.</font>

47
00:02:56,610 --> 00:03:02,508
<font color="#000000">To rule out leakage they test filtered Chameleon
and Squirrel, and for scale,</font>

48
00:03:02,588 --> 00:03:08,330
<font color="#000000">mini-batch experiments on three graphs with
hundreds of thousands of nodes.</font>

49
00:03:08,710 --> 00:03:11,171
<font color="#000000">Results are strong across the board.</font>

50
00:03:11,251 --> 00:03:16,747
<font color="#000000">On node classification, CoCN gets the best average
rank, one point eight three,</font>

51
00:03:16,827 --> 00:03:21,618
<font color="#000000">topping Chameleon at seventy-nine percent, Squirrel
at seventy-three,</font>

52
00:03:21,698 --> 00:03:23,453
<font color="#000000">and Cornell at eighty-six.</font>

53
00:03:23,533 --> 00:03:27,265
<font color="#000000">On graph classification, it wins five of six datasets,</font>

54
00:03:27,345 --> 00:03:29,523
<font color="#000000">including COLLAB and IMDB-Multi.</font>

55
00:03:29,603 --> 00:03:34,041
<font color="#000000">And on filtered Chameleon and Squirrel, with
duplicates removed,</font>

56
00:03:34,121 --> 00:03:38,770
<font color="#000000">it ranks first of eighteen models, so its edge is
not data leakage.</font>

57
00:03:39,150 --> 00:03:42,676
<font color="#000000">Several ablations show why CoCN works.</font>

58
00:03:42,756 --> 00:03:46,281
<font color="#000000">First, permutation heads matter: with more permutations,</font>

59
00:03:46,361 --> 00:03:49,887
<font color="#000000">accuracy rises consistently on MUTAG, Chameleon,
Cornell, and Wisconsin,</font>

60
00:03:49,967 --> 00:03:53,492
<font color="#000000">since each exposes different node arrangements.</font>

61
00:03:53,572 --> 00:03:57,098
<font color="#000000">Second, larger diagonal kernels give a bigger
receptive field and better performance,</font>

62
00:03:57,178 --> 00:04:00,703
<font color="#000000">without over-smoothing.</font>

63
00:04:00,783 --> 00:04:04,309
<font color="#000000">Third,</font>

64
00:04:04,389 --> 00:04:07,914
<font color="#000000">an information-loss study finds the relaxed
permutations preserve almost all information,</font>

65
00:04:07,994 --> 00:04:11,520
<font color="#000000">so the regularization is lossless.</font>

66
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:17,395
<font color="#000000">CoCN reaches an average rank of one point eight
three across six node datasets,</font>

67
00:04:17,475 --> 00:04:19,018
<font color="#000000">the best of any method.</font>

68
00:04:19,098 --> 00:04:22,617
<font color="#000000">On Squirrel it scores nearly seventy-three percent,</font>

69
00:04:22,697 --> 00:04:26,216
<font color="#000000">over eleven points above the strongest prior model.</font>

70
00:04:26,296 --> 00:04:28,827
<font color="#000000">On COLLAB it hits eighty-six percent.</font>

71
00:04:28,907 --> 00:04:34,120
<font color="#000000">And it cuts permutation modeling from intractable n
factorial to n squared.</font>

72
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:36,110
<font color="#000000">The takeaway is elegant.</font>

73
00:04:36,190 --> 00:04:41,957
<font color="#000000">Learn a differentiable permutation that arranges a
graph's nodes into a single row,</font>

74
00:04:42,037 --> 00:04:47,028
<font color="#000000">and all the machinery of image convolution becomes
available for graphs.</font>

75
00:04:47,108 --> 00:04:51,113
<font color="#000000">CoCN does exactly this, provably convergent and invariant,</font>

76
00:04:51,193 --> 00:04:53,508
<font color="#000000">reaching state-of-the-art results.</font>

77
00:04:53,588 --> 00:04:57,100
<font color="#000000">Graphs, all in a row, can be convolved like images.</font>
